@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Four interconnected blocks demonstrating proxy ports,
'                 full ports, flow ports, an interface block typing several
'                 ports, and flow properties with direction.
'   usecase:      Teach port kinds (proxy/full/flow), interface blocks,
'                 and flow-property direction (in/out/inout).
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       sysml internal block definition / interface blocks
' ============================================================================
title Example Internal BDD - Interface Blocks & Port Kinds

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Mapping note
'   - block              -> class <<block>>
'   - interface block    -> class <<interfaceBlock>> (types one or more ports)
'   - proxy port         -> port drawn on boundary, provides the interface
'                           block's features by delegation to an inner part
'   - full port          -> port owned by the block (part-typed), owns behavior
'   - flow port          -> port carrying a typed item flow
'   - flow property      -> property with direction in / out / inout inside
'                           the interface block; shown in its body
'   - connector          -> --> edge; the label names the item flow type and
'                           the flow properties conveyed
' An INTERNAL-BDD shows the internals of multiple block definitions at once
' and the item flows that cross between them. This is more abstract than a
' single-block IBD (which zooms into ONE block's interior).
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note over "SourceBlock", "IProcessor"
  Proxy ports (drawn open) provide IProcessor
  features by delegation. Full ports (solid)
  own their behavior. Flow ports (()) carry
  typed item flows. The interface block
  IProcessor types several ports at once and
  declares flow properties with direction.
end note

' Interface block typing the proxy ports of SourceBlock and SinkBlock and
' the flow port between ProcessorBlock and CoolerBlock.
class "IProcessor" <<interfaceBlock>> {
  +fluidIn : FluidFlow { direction = in }
  +fluidOut : FluidFlow { direction = out }
  +control : ControlSignal { direction = inout }
}

class "SourceBlock" <<block>> {
  +outProxy : IProcessor   '<<proxy>>
  +emitter : FluidStore
}

class "ProcessorBlock" <<block>> {
  +inProxy : IProcessor    '<<proxy>>
  +inFlow : FluidPort      '<<flow>>
  +outFlow : FluidPort     '<<flow>>
  +controller : FullController '<<full>>
  part core : CoreUnit
}

class "CoolerBlock" <<block>> {
  +inFlow : FluidPort      '<<flow>>
  +sinkProxy : IProcessor  '<<proxy>>
  part radiator : Radiator
}

' Proxy-to-proxy delegation through the interface block.
' The item flow on the connector is typed by FluidFlow and carries the
' flow properties declared in IProcessor.
"SourceBlock" -->[color=#darkgreen] "ProcessorBlock" : <inProxy.out> FluidFlow { rate, temperature }

' Flow-port to flow-port connector inside the multi-block internal view.
"ProcessorBlock" -(#darkblue)->[color=#darkblue] "CoolerBlock" : FluidFlow { rate, temperature }

' Control-signal flow property is bidirectional (inout).
"SourceBlock" <.[color=orange].> "ProcessorBlock" : ControlSignal { command, ack }

note right of "IProcessor"
  INTERFACE BLOCK types multiple ports
  across block boundaries.
  Flow properties declare direction:
    in   = received
    out  = sent
    inout = bidirectional
end note

note bottom of "ProcessorBlock"
  ProcessorBlock.controller is a FULL PORT
  (part-typed, owns behavior). inProxy is a
  PROXY PORT delegating to core. inFlow and
  outFlow are FLOW PORTS carrying FluidFlow.
end note

@enduml
